╭─ VULN-0001 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Request payload silently dropped when retransmitting                 │
│  Uri-Path-Abbrev requests (coap_net.c)                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: LOW                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 3.7                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: libcoap 4.3.5 (source snapshot)                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: coap_send() → coap_net.c (Uri-Path-Abbrev expansion path)         │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: CoAP (UDP datagram generated by the library)                        │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  When a CoAP session has learned that the peer does not understand the       │
│  Uri-Path-Abbrev option, `coap_send()` rebuilds outgoing requests with the   │
│  abbreviation expanded. The rebuilt PDU is created correctly, but the        │
│  original message payload is appended to the *old* PDU, which is then        │
│  freed, so the request that reaches the wire carries no payload. Any         │
│  body-bearing PUT/POST issued on such a session is delivered to the server   │
│  without its content.                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Integrity of client requests is silently altered: a request issued with     │
│  content is transmitted without any content. For a resource whose handler    │
│  treats an empty body as "replace with nothing" (or which deletes on empty   │
│  PUT), the effect is data loss on the server; for any other handler the      │
│  request semantics change without the application being informed. In a       │
│  proxy chain the truncated request is relayed upstream as-is, so the same    │
│  loss propagates. No confidentiality or availability impact was              │
│  demonstrated.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  `coap_send_internal()` contains a fallback for peers that rejected the      │
│  Uri-Path-Abbrev option. Once `session->no_path_abbrev` is set (it is set    │
│  by the library when the server answers `4.02 Bad Option` to an abbreviated  │
│  request), every subsequent request carrying a Uri-Path-Abbrev option is     │
│  duplicated with `coap_pdu_duplicate_lkd(..., COAP_BOOL_TRUE)`, which        │
│  expands the abbreviation into Uri-Path options. That function explicitly    │
│  copies only the token and options, not the payload:                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=coap_pdu.c startLineNumber=233 endLineNumber=235                 │
│  /*                                                                          │
│   * Note: This does not include any data, just the token and options         │
│   */                                                                         │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The payload therefore has to be re-added to the *new* PDU. The code         │
│  instead appends it to the source PDU:                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/coap_net.c startLineNumber=1802 endLineNumber=1808           │
│        new = coap_pdu_duplicate_lkd(pdu, session, pdu->actual_token.length,  │
│                                     pdu->actual_token.s, NULL,               │
│  COAP_BOOL_TRUE);                                                            │
│        if (new) {                                                            │
│          if (coap_get_data(pdu, &data_len, &data)) {                         │
│            coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);                               │
│          }                                                                   │
│          coap_log_debug("*  Retransmitting PDU with Uri-Path-Abbrev          │
│  replaced (3)\n");                                                           │
│          coap_delete_pdu_lkd(pdu);                                           │
│          pdu = new;                                                          │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `pdu` is the PDU that is deleted two lines later, so the data is written    │
│  to freed-and-discarded storage, while `new` (the PDU that is actually       │
│  transmitted) never receives a payload. The call also emits `coap_add_data:  │
│  PDU already contains data`, because the source PDU already held that        │
│  payload.                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  The identical construct elsewhere in the library writes to the new PDU and  │
│  is correct, confirming the intended behaviour:                              │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=5191 endLineNumber=5199         │
│            pdu = coap_pdu_duplicate_lkd(lg_crcv->sent_pdu, session,          │
│  ltoken_len,                                                                 │
│                                         ltoken, NULL, COAP_BOOL_TRUE);       │
│          if (pdu) {                                                          │
│            if (coap_get_data(lg_crcv->sent_pdu, &data_len, &data)) {         │
│              coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);                             │
│            }                                                                 │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Reachability: the fallback runs only for sessions where the library's       │
│  block layer is active (`session->block_mode & COAP_BLOCK_USE_LIBCOAP`) and  │
│  `session->no_path_abbrev` has been set, which requires the application to   │
│  use the Uri-Path-Abbrev facility (`coap_path_into_optlist_abbrev()` /       │
│  `coap_uri_into_optlist_abbrev()` with a mapping). A remote CoAP server (or  │
│  an on-path attacker able to rewrite server responses) can force the         │
│  transition by answering `4.02` to the first abbreviated request, after      │
│  which every abbreviated body-bearing request from that client is silently   │
│  sent without its payload.                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Bind a local UDP socket to act as the upstream CoAP server and note its  │
│  port.                                                                       │
│  2. Create a libcoap client session to that port with                        │
│  `COAP_BLOCK_USE_LIBCOAP` enabled and `session->no_path_abbrev = 1`, which   │
│  is the state the library itself enters after the server answers `4.02` to   │
│  a Uri-Path-Abbrev request.                                                  │
│  3. Register a Uri-Path-Abbrev mapping (`coap_upa_server_mapping()`), then   │
│  build a `PUT` with one Uri-Path-Abbrev option and the 4-byte payload        │
│  `BODY`, and pass it to `coap_send()`.                                       │
│  4. Read the datagram that the library actually transmitted.                 │
│  5. Observe that the abbreviated option has been expanded to `Uri-Path: a`,  │
│  `Uri-Path: b`, but the message ends after the options: there is no `0xFF`   │
│  payload marker and no `BODY`, so the payload was lost.                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Running the same program against a library with `coap_add_data(new, ...)`   │
│  at the reported line shows the payload present (`... 0x0c 0x70 0x29 0x34    │
│  0x93 0xff 'B''O''D''Y'`).                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```c                                                                        │
│  #include "coap3/coap_internal.h"                                            │
│  #include <stdio.h>                                                          │
│  #include <string.h>                                                         │
│  #include <arpa/inet.h>                                                      │
│  #include <sys/socket.h>                                                     │
│  #include <unistd.h>                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  int main(void) {                                                            │
│    int rx;                                                                   │
│    struct sockaddr_in sin;                                                   │
│    socklen_t slen = sizeof(sin);                                             │
│    coap_context_t *ctx;                                                      │
│    coap_session_t *session;                                                  │
│    coap_address_t dst;                                                       │
│    coap_pdu_t *pdu;                                                          │
│    uint8_t buf[1500];                                                        │
│    ssize_t n;                                                                │
│    static coap_upa_abbrev_t map[] = { { 1, "a/b" } };                        │
│                                                                              │
│    /* local receiver standing in for the upstream CoAP server */             │
│    rx = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);                                      │
│    memset(&sin, 0, sizeof(sin));                                             │
│    sin.sin_family = AF_INET;                                                 │
│    sin.sin_addr.s_addr = htonl(INADDR_LOOPBACK);                             │
│    sin.sin_port = 0;                                                         │
│    bind(rx, (struct sockaddr *)&sin, sizeof(sin));                           │
│    getsockname(rx, (struct sockaddr *)&sin, &slen);                          │
│                                                                              │
│    coap_startup();                                                           │
│    ctx = coap_new_context(NULL);                                             │
│    coap_address_init(&dst);                                                  │
│    dst.addr.sin = sin;                                                       │
│    dst.size = sizeof(sin);                                                   │
│    session = coap_new_client_session(ctx, NULL, &dst, COAP_PROTO_UDP);       │
│    session->state = COAP_SESSION_STATE_ESTABLISHED;                          │
│    /* state reached after the server answered 4.02 to a Uri-Path-Abbrev      │
│  request */                                                                  │
│    session->block_mode |= COAP_BLOCK_USE_LIBCOAP;                            │
│    session->no_path_abbrev = 1;                                              │
│    coap_upa_server_mapping(map, 1);                                          │
│                                                                              │
│    pdu = coap_pdu_init(COAP_MESSAGE_CON, COAP_REQUEST_CODE_PUT,              │
│                        coap_new_message_id(session), 1152);                  │
│    coap_add_token(pdu, 1, (const uint8_t *)"a");                             │
│    coap_add_option(pdu, COAP_OPTION_URI_PATH_ABB, 1, (const uint8_t          │
│  *)"\x01");                                                                  │
│    coap_add_data(pdu, 4, (const uint8_t *)"BODY");                           │
│    coap_send(session, pdu);                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│    n = recv(rx, buf, sizeof(buf), 0);                                        │
│    if (n < 0) { perror("recv"); return 2; }                                  │
│    printf("wire bytes: ");                                                   │
│    for (ssize_t i = 0; i < n; i++) printf("%02x", buf[i]);                   │
│    printf("\npayload present: %s\n",                                         │
│           memmem(buf, (size_t)n, "BODY", 4) ? "YES" : "NO (payload lost)");  │
│    close(rx);                                                                │
│    return 0;                                                                 │
│  }                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: src/coap_net.c:1805                                           │
│    Copy the request payload into the duplicated PDU instead of the           │
│  discarded source PDU                                                        │
│              coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);                             │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -           coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);                           │
│    +           coap_add_data(new, data_len, data);                           │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: src/coap_block.c:5197                                         │
│    Informational: the equivalent block-layer fallback correctly adds the     │
│  payload to the duplicated PDU (there `pdu` is the new PDU)                  │
│                coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Add the request payload to the duplicated PDU that is actually              │
│  transmitted, not to the source PDU that is deleted immediately afterwards.  │
│  The duplicated PDU deliberately carries only the token and options, so the  │
│  payload must be re-added explicitly after the duplication succeeds and      │
│  before the source PDU is released. Consider adding a regression test that   │
│  sends a body-bearing request through the Uri-Path-Abbrev fallback and       │
│  asserts the payload arrives, since no existing unit test covers this        │
│  branch.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
