╭─ VULN-0002 (updated) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Unauthenticated memory exhaustion in OSCORE Appendix B.2 context     │
│  handling                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 5.9                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: libcoap 4.3.5 OSCORE (src/coap_oscore.c,                            │
│  src/oscore/oscore_context.c)                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: CoAP/UDP OSCORE request carrying an OSCORE option with a          │
│  kid_context                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: POST                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  When an OSCORE security context has RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 enabled, libcoap  │
│  derives and permanently links a new OSCORE security context for every       │
│  incoming request that carries a previously unseen `kid_context`, and it     │
│  does so **before** the AEAD tag is verified. When verification fails        │
│  (always, for a peer without the master secret) the newly created context    │
│  is never removed. An unauthenticated attacker can therefore make the        │
│  server allocate an unbounded number of security contexts by sending         │
│  requests with ever-changing `kid_context` values, eventually exhausting     │
│  memory and taking the CoAP service down.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Remote, unauthenticated denial of service. Each crafted datagram            │
│  permanently adds one OSCORE security context (master secret/salt copies,    │
│  sender and recipient contexts, derived keys) to the CoAP context chain.     │
│  Memory grows without bound and is never reclaimed until the process         │
│  restarts; because context lookup walks the whole chain, request processing  │
│  cost also degrades quadratically as the chain grows. On the constrained     │
│  devices libcoap targets, a few thousand packets are enough to exhaust the   │
│  available heap.                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  Request processing in `coap_oscore_decrypt_pdu()` performs all of the       │
│  following **before** `cose_encrypt0_decrypt()` is reached:                  │
│                                                                              │
│  1. `oscore_decode_option_value()` extracts `kid` and `kid_context` from     │
│  the OSCORE option.                                                          │
│  2. `oscore_find_context()` fails to match the supplied `kid_context`.       │
│  3. Because the security context has `rfc8613_b_2` set, the code             │
│  CBOR-unwraps the attacker-supplied `kid_context` and calls                  │
│  `oscore_duplicate_ctx()` (src/coap_oscore.c:1111).                          │
│  4. `oscore_duplicate_ctx()` allocates a new `oscore_ctx_t`, copies the      │
│  master secret/salt, derives a sender key and a common IV, adds a recipient  │
│  for the supplied `kid`, and links the context into the CoAP context chain   │
│  with `oscore_enter_context()` (src/oscore/oscore_context.c).                │
│                                                                              │
│  The AEAD check then fails, and the error path at                            │
│  src/coap_oscore.c:1856-1867 deletes the request association and the PDUs    │
│  but never unlinks or frees the duplicated context. `osc_ctx` is a local     │
│  variable that goes out of scope. The context is only ever freed by          │
│  `oscore_remove_context()`/`coap_free_context()`, so it stays in the CoAP    │
│  context chain forever.                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Because `oscore_find_context()` iterates the whole context chain for every  │
│  request, the per-request cost grows linearly with the number of             │
│  accumulated contexts, so the flood is also a CPU amplification vector.      │
│                                                                              │
│  The defect is the ordering of "create security state" before "authenticate  │
│  the message", combined with a failure path that does not roll the state     │
│  back. The `kid_context` bytes are fully attacker-controlled (1-250 bytes),  │
│  so both the number of contexts and their size can be driven by the          │
│  attacker.                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Configure an OSCORE security context with `master_secret`,               │
│  `master_salt`, a `sender_id`, at least one `recipient_id`, and              │
│  `rfc8613_b_2,bool,true`; start the shipped `coap-server` with that          │
│  configuration on a local UDP port.                                          │
│  2. Send a CoAP NON POST datagram to the server. The PDU contains a single   │
│  OSCORE option (number 9) whose value is `0x19` (Partial IV length 1, H and  │
│  K bits set), a one-byte Partial IV, the `kid_context` length byte, the      │
│  CBOR-wrapped `kid_context`, and the recipient key id `0x01`; the payload    │
│  is arbitrary ciphertext.                                                    │
│  3. Vary the `kid_context` bytes in every datagram so each request names a   │
│  context the server has not seen before. No valid AEAD tag is ever           │
│  supplied, so every request is rejected with 4.01.                           │
│  4. Observe the server's resident memory grow linearly with the number of    │
│  packets and never recover; with `rfc8613_b_2,bool,false` the identical      │
│  traffic leaves the memory flat.                                             │
│  5. The same effect can be measured deterministically by walking the CoAP    │
│  context's linked OSCORE context chain and counting the contexts before and  │
│  after the flood.                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  # Unauthenticated OSCORE context accumulation against libcoap (RFC 8613     │
│  App. B.2)                                                                   │
│  # Server: coap-server -A 127.0.0.1 -p 5683 -E b2.conf                       │
│  # b2.conf:                                                                  │
│  #   master_secret,hex,"0102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f10"                    │
│  #   master_salt,hex,"9e7ca92223786340"                                      │
